        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-purpose-and-boundary-those-concerns-belong-to-15": {
      "id": "b-10-purpose-and-boundary-those-concerns-belong-to-15",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Those concerns belong to a kernel or host application. A kernel imports\nprompt-kit, renders prompt documents, and wires them into its own runtime\npacket. Prompt-kit remains reusable because it does not know which runtime\nconsumes the rendered prompt. The agent host in "
        },
        {
          "insert": "packages/prompt-kit-agent",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " is\none such consumer, and the seam between the two is specified in\n"
        },
        {
          "insert": "Kernel Boundary",
          "attributes": {
            "reference": {
              "kind": "doc",
              "path": "10-system-design/80-kernel-boundary"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-purpose-and-boundary-source-of-truth-16": {
      "id": "b-10-purpose-and-boundary-source-of-truth-16",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Source Of Truth"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-purpose-and-boundary-the-canonical-source-is-17": {
      "id": "b-10-purpose-and-boundary-the-canonical-source-is-17",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The canonical source is the structured prompt document. Rendered text is an\noutput artifact, not the authored source."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-purpose-and-boundary-this-matters-for-authori-18": {
      "id": "b-10-purpose-and-boundary-this-matters-for-authori-18",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "This matters for authoring tools. A prompt viewer can display the rendered\ntext, but editing must commit to the structured document. Hand-editing the\nrendered text creates a second source of truth and breaks transforms,\nvalidation, and stable ids. The editor follows this rule by presenting the\nrendered output as an editable projection while committing every gesture to the\nstructured document; its design lives in\n"
        },
        {
          "insert": "60-editor/",
          "attributes": {
            "reference": {
              "kind": "doc",
              "path": "10-system-design/60-editor"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "mcp-add-0": {
      "id": "mcp-add-0",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Shared Authoring"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "mcp-add-1": {
      "id": "mcp-add-1",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Humans and external agents should work on one canonical prompt with the same persistence guarantees. Direct edits need current writing guidance, a visible production render, revision checks, and recoverable history; they do not require a second model to mediate every save."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    }
  }
